# Config setup

Use a single configuration approach across backend and frontend to keep behavior consistent.
The structure stays the same in both runtimes; only the config values and sources differ by context.

## Why this pattern

Using the same lookup shape in both runtimes makes config behavior predictable.
It reduces drift between frontend and backend, makes environment switching easier,
and keeps new config variants simple to add without changing call sites.

## Shared pattern

```ts
// config.ts (shared idea)
import type { Config } from './createConfig';
import { defaultConfig } from './items/default';
import { stagingConfig } from './items/staging';

export const configs: Record<string, Config> = {
	default: defaultConfig,
	staging: stagingConfig,
};

export const getConfig = (selected?: string) => {
	return configs[selected || 'default'];
};

export const config = getConfig();
```

## Frontend example

```ts
// frontend/src/config/config.ts
import type { Config } from './createConfig';
import { PUBLIC_SELECTED_CONFIG } from '$env/static/public';
import { defaultConfig } from './items/default';
import { stagingConfig } from './items/staging';

export const configs: Record<string, Config> = {
	default: defaultConfig,
	staging: stagingConfig,
};

export const getConfig = () => {
	return configs[PUBLIC_SELECTED_CONFIG || 'default'];
};

export const config = getConfig();
```

## Backend example

```ts
// backend/src/config/config.ts
import { Config } from './createConfig';
import { defaultConfig } from './items/default';
import { stagingConfig } from './items/staging';

