# Payload Types

We want to use objects in the front-end without too much trouble, so we're going to make sure we can export and import payload types. The types are generated based on your setup of collections and globals in your payload project. Never edit these types manually.

### Generating the types
First, we generate the payload types! We do this by running the following command in the `backend/` directory:

```bash
pnpm generate:types
```

This command will generate a file called `payload-types.ts` inside the `backend/src` directory.
```Ts
export interface User {
    id: string;
    updatedAt: string;
    createdAt: string;
    email: string;
    resetPasswordToken?: string | null;
    resetPasswordExpiration?: string | null;
    salt?: string | null;
    hash?: string | null;
    loginAttempts?: number| null;
    lockUntil?: string | null;
    password?: string | null;
}
```
This is the default `User` type payload generates when running the types command, its objects serves as a blueprint for creating actual user objects. Now that we have this type, we can create a user by assigning values to each of its properties. For example, if we’re building a registration form, we’ll use this type to ensure we collect and store all the required information correctly.

You can view all the generated types in the file: `backend/src/payload-types.ts`. This file contains definitions for different data structures used in our project.
### Linking the Backend as a Dev Dependency in the Frontend
To enable the frontend to import Payload types from the backend, we'll add the backend as a dev dependency in the frontend project.

Now to link it we need to add the dependencies party into the `frontend/package.json`, do this by adding the `backend` script into the devDependencies section.

```json
"devDependencies": {
      "": "...",
      "backend": "workspace:backend"
}
```

Now after that's finished we can run `pnpm install` in the frontend and we're done.
### Create a Type Export File in the Frontend
Now we're going to create a utility file in the `frontend/src/lib` folder called **payload-types.ts**. Here we can easily import the payload types in and export them to other frontend files.

```typescript
import * as PayloadTypes from 'backend/src/payload-types';
export { PayloadTypes };
```

This is especially really nice because we never have to duplicate the types, since it's all handled in the backend and directly imported in the frontend.

:::note Note:
If this code has an error in the path string in one of these lines, you should then check the directories in which you executed the previous steps and change the path based on that.
:::

# Example

At the example below you can see, that the same block can be used several times, so to not just duplicate code, we can make a payload type called "Article" that will include Title, main text and an arrow, and then use in the frontend section just apply unique text for every single article.

## Example: Using the `Article` Payload Type

Suppose you have the following type generated in your backend:

```typescript
// backend/src/payload-types.ts
export interface Article {
  title: string;
  text: string;
  showArrow: boolean;
}
```

In your frontend, you can import and use this type as follows:

```typescript
// frontend/src/lib/payload-types.ts
import * as PayloadTypes from 'backend/src/payload-types';
export { PayloadTypes };
```

Now, in a Svelte or TypeScript file in your frontend:

```typescript
import { PayloadTypes } from '../lib/payload-types';

const articles: PayloadTypes.Article[] = [
  {
    title: 'Getting Started',
    text: 'Learn how to set up your project quickly.',
    showArrow: true,
  },
  {
    title: 'Advanced Features',
    text: 'Explore advanced options and customization.',
    showArrow: false,
  },
];

// You can now use the `articles` array in your components
```

This way, you avoid duplicating type definitions and always use the latest types from your backend.

